    "id": "deviantart:588062628",
    "source": "deviantart",
    "type": "post",
    "title": "Khamsin Refinery Complex",
    "text": "In the seemingly lifeless shadow of an immense canyon among the scorched badlands, a massive, brutalist facility serves to extract precious materials from far below the burning sands. Scout vehicles sent to oversee the state of the facility's exterior look no more than mere ants to a viewer atop one of the facility's serpentine decks and walkways. Dozens more of these collosal complexes exist within the dead, arid region.\n\nIt was quite literally supposed to be just a speedy, but it ended up taking much more than that! Probably more or less 24 hours of work, spread over perhaps two weeks. Slightly inspired by many artworks I've recently spotted once more from Akira and Blade Runner. Those two have quite a lot in common - at least in background materials.  I might not release too much works in this level of detail, it tends to eat up the pace. \n\nInitially, the name of the picture was 'Death Processing Facility Z', from a novice-built deathmatch map submitted to a map collection site a long, long, long time ago for the first Unreal Tournament. A lot of commenters lambasted the map badly for being simplistic, ugly, and composed mostly of thin, empty cubes. One commenter asked \"How the heck does a facility process DEATH?\". Another questioned \"If it's Z, that means there are also facilities A-Y that process death?!\".\n\nI believe they were wrong in attacking the concept (I'm not serious, of course!) - I thought to myself, what would my own take on a Death Processing Facility Z look like?! Definitely huge and menacing, as such, that was the theme I was going for with large, geometric, unfriendly shapes as a structural theme. \n\nI still don't know how the facility processes death though but since it's a refinery and we can assume it refines oil, I guess there's some dots to connect.",
    "url": "https://www.deviantart.com/ungdi-sea/art/Khamsin-Refinery-Complex-588062628",
    "published_at": "2016-02-01T14:03:03.000Z",
    "fetched_at": "2026-11-25T20:23:05.222Z"
  }
